Score: 8.5/10
This is a solid REST API implementation! You've successfully transitioned from console applications to web services and covered all the required functionality.
What you executed well:
Complete REST endpoints - All five required endpoints are implemented with proper HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE). Your URL mapping is clean and follows RESTful conventions.
Good separation of concerns - Your controller focuses on HTTP concerns while delegating business logic to the service layer. The integration between your existing JDBC layer and the new REST layer is seamless.
Practical endpoint design - Having both /courses for database operations and /courses/discover for the recommender service is a smart way to demonstrate both functionalities.
Proper HTTP status codes - You're returning appropriate status codes (201 for created, 404 for not found, etc.) and meaningful response messages.
Areas that need attention:
Missing Swagger implementation - This was specifically requested but I don't see the Swagger UI configuration or annotations. You've got the SpringDoc dependency but no @Operation, @ApiResponse, or other OpenAPI annotations on your endpoints.
Inconsistent error handling - Your getCourseById method has null checking, but your repository's findById will throw an exception if no course exists. This could cause 500 errors instead of clean 404s.
Mixing concerns in one endpoint - Your /courses/discover endpoint returns Course objects with database-style IDs, but these are actually mock data from your recommenders. This might confuse API consumers.
Quick improvements:
Add Swagger annotations - Put @Operation(summary = "Get all courses") and similar annotations on your endpoints to fulfill the Swagger requirement.
Handle database exceptions - Wrap your repository calls in try-catch blocks to convert SQL exceptions into proper HTTP responses.
Consider response DTOs - Maybe separate your database Course model from your API responses, especially for the recommender endpoints.
The core functionality is solid though. This feels like a working REST API that could handle real requests. You've made good progress from console apps to web services!
